00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.050
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:18.550
So we switch gears now and talk
about quantum electrodynamics,

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:19.050
QED.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.930
And we start the
discussion by going back

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.250
to free wave equations.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.190
Now could argue that we are
interested in collisions

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.210
and we're interested
in decays of particles.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.960
So why do we discuss
free wave equations?

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.010
But the theory we
discussed last week,

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.410
which we used in order to get
a hold on Feynman diagrams

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.690
and calculations,
was very simplified.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.720
And one of the aspects not
considered in this theory

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.540
was the fact that
particles carry spin.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.965
So we had a theory which not
only was applicable to scalars.

00:00:49.965 --> 00:00:52.660
Now by walking through
wave equations,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:57.810
we can see how we can
incorporate or make

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.720
use of the fact that particles
actually do carry spin.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.950
So let's do this one by one.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.150
So we start off with our
relativistic energy-momentum

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.020
relation--

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.590
e squared is equal to p
squared plus m squared.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.620
We express energy
and momentum via

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.630
quantum mechanical operators.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.490
And so immediately
by putting this in,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.710
we find this
equation here, which

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.370
is the so-called
Klein-Gordon wave equation.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.300
So if you look at
this equation, we

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.430
see that the second
derivative here in time,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.790
there's no derivative in space.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.990
So there's an asymmetry
between space and time.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.850
And that is a not really useful
feature of our wave equation

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:44.760
as we want them to be, Lorentz
invariant, for example.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.310
So what we want is a first-order
equation in both derivatives.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.990
So we'll just start writing
this down in general terms,

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.370
and then make sure that
this equation holds

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.310
to the relativistic
information we just

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.077
saw on the previous slide.

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:03.410
We'll just write this down here.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.956
We have a first derivative time
and a first derivative space.

00:02:06.956 --> 00:02:09.289
And we'll just say there's a
constant between those two,

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.200
relating those two.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.810
So the sigmas are just
unknown constants.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.020
So if you now try to find
by squaring this, trying

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.505
to find the
Klein-Gordon equation

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:21.980
and relates the
coefficients, you

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.930
find this relationship here.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.110
So the sigma squared are
all the same and equal to 1.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.400
But you also see
that the sigmas,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.500
they're anti-commutate-- sorry--

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.750
which is not
possible for numbers.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.730
So sigmas need to be matrices.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.150
You also see that this
is only holding true here

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.920
for m equal to 0.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.415
So this equation here is
true for a massless particle.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.030
All right, so if we then
try to find solutions

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.180
for those relations, we find
that they can be fulfilled

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.820
by the 2 by 2 Pauli matrices.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.140
We might have seen
this already hopefully

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:09.260
in the discussion
of atomic physics.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:16.950
And there, those Pauli matrices
associate spin to electrons.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.370
So this is exactly what
we have in mind here also.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.250
Now using this definition, we
can rewrite the Weyl equations

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.630
to energy times the
field is equal to minus

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.150
sigma times the momentum
times the field.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.506
And to find a second equation,
we'll just design flips.

00:03:37.506 --> 00:03:40.700
The chi here and
the phi spinors,

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.190
they're two-dimensional
vectors and the sigma

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.590
are our Pauli matrices.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.140
Good.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.620
So we have the relation
of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.170
So we can go a step further.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.933
Now we want to introduce
mass term as well.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.350
Those hold for
massless particles,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.470
so we're going introduce mass.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.620
So we can rewrite this equation
and introduce its mass term

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.940
here, again, with
the coefficient.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:09.170
And we find now this
alpha here being--

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.070
sorry.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.940
So this phi here is a
core component spinor.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.480
And it stands for the particle,
its antiparticle, and the two

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:18.110
spin states.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.169
So that's combining that
two equations we had here.

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:22.700
So you'll see one
is for particles

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.120
and one is for antiparticles,
for the two spin states.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.660
So we combine this
in one equation

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.310
and we added this mass term.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.310
So if you try to find
the solutions here,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:35.150
you find that
alpha is a matrix--

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.640
4 by 4 matrix which has the
sigma, supporting matrices

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.510
on the off-diagonal elements.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.330
And beta is a
diagonal matrix-- a 4

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:48.660
by 4 matrix with identity
on the upper two components,

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.960
and minus 1 in the
lower two components.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.270
So now with this, this is
already the Dirac equation.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.360
We can rewrite the Dirac
equation in the covariant form

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.120
where you have just
defined a new matrix here,

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.390
so-called gamma matrix
which you build out

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.360
of this matrix beta and alpha.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.290
Which are defined on
the previous slide.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.100
Good, so good.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.800
So we have this new
matrix, this new equation

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.340
here, which is the
Dirac equation.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.450
And it holds for particles
with two spin states, examples

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.920
with spin half states.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.593
And it holds for particles
which have masses.

00:05:26.593 --> 00:05:27.260
So that's great.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.690
So this is now on starting
point for the discussion.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:34.460
The next lecture we'll look
at solutions of this equation.

